WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.579
Idea. When you type a message, the computer cannot store letters. It can only store binary. So everyone agrees a list that gives each character its own number. That list is called a character set.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:25.319
Standard ASCII uses seven bits for each character. Two to the power seven is one hundred and twenty-eight, so there are one hundred and twenty-eight codes, from nought to one hundred and twenty-seven.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:35.643
The letters run in order. If capital A is sixty-five, then B is sixty-six, and so on. To find a code, count on from the one you are given.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:51.047
Unicode is a much bigger character set. Its encodings use more bits for many characters, sixteen or more, so it can cover almost every language, plus symbols and emoji. Its first one hundred and twenty-eight codes match ASCII.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:01:02.741
Method. To find a code, count on from the code you are given. Then, if the question asks, write it as seven-bit binary using the headers sixty-four down to one.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:14.507
To explain why Unicode was needed, make a point and link it to a reason. Unicode covers other languages, because it uses more bits, so it has far more codes than ASCII.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:28.991
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper 1 in June 2022. Explain one reason to use Unicode instead of ASCII for languages other than English. It is worth two marks.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:44.201
The point: languages such as Arabic, Chinese or Urdu use many characters that are not English letters. ASCII has only seven bits, which is one hundred and twenty-eight codes. That is enough for English, but not for them.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:02:00.767
The reason: Unicode uses sixteen or more bits, which gives sixty-five thousand five hundred and thirty-six codes or more. So every character can have its own code. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:14.037
Common mistake. A common mistake is saying standard ASCII uses eight bits, with two hundred and fifty-six characters. That is extended ASCII. Standard ASCII is seven bits.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:22.918
Another mistake is counting one too many. H is seven letters after A, not eight. Count the steps between, not the letters.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:35.162
Your turn. Now you try. In ASCII, small a is ninety-seven. What is the code for small e? Give it in denary and in seven-bit binary. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:51.645
Answer. Small e is four steps after small a, so ninety-seven plus four is one hundred and one. One hundred and one is sixty-four plus thirty-two plus four plus one. The answer is one one zero zero one zero one.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:05.085
Remember the method: ASCII is seven bits and one hundred and twenty-eight codes; count on to find a code; and for Unicode, give a point with a linked reason. Now try the practice questions for this type.
